        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Created**: 2026-02-03\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Custom full parse-to-object implementation | Bun has no built-in YAML parser; external dependencies prohibited by REQ-008; full parsing provides thorough validation | Minimal syntax checker (faster but less thorough), external library like js-yaml (violates REQ-008) |\n| D2 | Parser architecture | Two-phase: Lexer + Recursive Descent Parser | Clean separation of concerns; lexer handles indentation tracking; parser focuses on structure; easier to maintain and debug | Single-pass parser (more complex state management), regex-based parsing (fragile for nested structures) |\n| D3 | File organization | `src/yaml-parser.ts` and `src/validate.ts` | Follows existing project pattern with source files in `src/` directory; separates reusable parser from CLI-specific code | Separate `cli/` or `lib/` directories (no precedent in codebase), single file (poor separation) |\n| D4 | Test framework | bun:test | Existing project pattern established in `utils.test.ts`; zero additional dependencies | Jest or Vitest (would require adding dependencies) |\n| D5 | Error reporting detail | Include line and column numbers in YamlParseError | Helps developers quickly locate syntax issues; standard practice for parsers | Message-only errors (less actionable for users) |\n| D6 | Multiline string support | Include literal `\\|` and folded `>` block scalars | Common in configuration files for multi-line values; expected by users | Exclude multiline (reduced real-world compatibility) |\n| D7 | Inline collection support | Support flow sequences `[]` and flow mappings `{}` | Common shorthand in YAML configs; improves validation coverage | Block-only collections (reduced compatibility) |\n| D8 | Boolean value recognition | Accept `true`, `false`, `yes`, `no` | YAML 1.1 compatibility; common in existing configs | Strict YAML 1.2 only (`true`/`false`); broader set including `on`/`off` |\n| D9 | Indentation handling | Spaces only; reject tabs | YAML spec requires spaces for indentation; tabs cause ambiguity | Allow tabs (violates YAML spec), convert tabs to spaces (implicit behavior) |\n| D10 | Empty file handling | Return `null` (valid per BR-002) | Business rule BR-002 specifies empty files are valid YAML; YAML spec allows empty documents | Reject empty files (violates requirements) |\n\n## Technology Selection Rationale\n\n### Why Custom Parser Instead of Dependency\n\nThe requirements explicitly prohibit new dependencies (REQ-008). While libraries like `js-yaml` or `yaml` would provide robust parsing, using them would violate project constraints. The custom parser:\n\n1. Maintains zero-dependency footprint\n2. Can be tailored to exact requirements (basic constructs only)\n3. Provides full control over error messages and behavior\n4. Keeps the implementation self-contained\n\n### Why Full Parse-to-Object vs Syntax Checker\n\nUser selected full parse-to-object approach during design session. Benefits:\n\n1. **Thorough validation**: Ensures the YAML can actually be deserialized, not just superficially valid\n2. **Type coercion verification**: Confirms scalars parse to expected types\n3. **Structure validation**: Verifies nested collections are properly formed\n4. **Future extensibility**: Parser output could be used for other features if needed\n\nTrade-offs accepted:\n- More complex implementation\n- Slightly longer execution time (still well under 1s requirement)\n\n### Why Lexer + Parser Architecture\n\nThe two-phase approach separates concerns:\n\n**Lexer responsibilities:**\n- Character-by-character processing\n- Indentation tracking with level stack\n- Token generation with position info\n- String literal handling (quotes, escapes)\n- Comment stripping\n\n**Parser responsibilities:**\n- Token stream consumption\n- Recursive structure building\n- Type inference for scalars\n- Error recovery and reporting\n\nThis separation makes the code more testable and maintainable than a single-pass approach.\n\n## Scope Boundaries\n\n### Explicitly Supported\n- Single-document YAML files\n- Common scalar types (string, number, boolean, null)\n- Block and flow collections\n- Comments (stripped during parsing)\n- Multiline strings (literal and folded)\n\n### Explicitly Not Supported (per requirements)\n- Anchors and aliases (`&name`, `*name`)\n- Custom tags (`!tag`)\n- Multiple documents (`---` separator)\n- Directives (`%YAML`, `%TAG`)\n- Merge keys (`<<`)\n- Complex mapping keys\n\nThese exclusions align with the \"Out of Scope\" section in requirements and keep the parser implementation manageable.\n"
