        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-03\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML file syntax to help developers quickly verify that their YAML configuration files are parseable before use in applications or deployments. The validator operates without introducing any new project dependencies.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to verify file syntax correctness. Invalid YAML can cause application failures, deployment issues, or debugging time spent on simple syntax errors. Currently, there is no built-in validation tool in the project.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- **Reduced debugging time**: Catch YAML syntax errors early before they cause runtime failures\n- **Improved developer experience**: Quick feedback loop for configuration file authoring\n- **Zero dependency footprint**: Maintains project simplicity by not adding external packages\n- **CI/CD integration ready**: Exit codes enable automated validation in pipelines\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | 100% correct identification of valid vs invalid YAML syntax |\n| Command execution time | Less than 1 second for typical config files (under 10KB) |\n| Exit code reliability | Consistent exit code 0 for valid, 1 for invalid YAML |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | Team members authoring or modifying YAML config files | Quick syntax validation before committing changes |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated build/deploy systems | Programmatic validation with reliable exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain project simplicity, no new dependencies to manage |\n| Project Maintainers | Consistent tooling patterns within the project |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Command-line interface accepting a file path argument\n- YAML syntax validation (parseability check)\n- Human-readable success/failure messages\n- Exit code 0 for valid YAML, exit code 1 for invalid YAML\n- Error messaging that indicates validation failure reason\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (checking YAML structure against a defined schema)\n- YAML formatting or auto-correction\n- Stdin input or string argument input methods\n- Multi-file batch validation\n- JSON output format\n- YAML-to-JSON conversion\n- Advanced YAML features validation (anchors, aliases, custom tags)\n- Watch mode or continuous validation\n- IDE/editor integrations\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime provides sufficient built-in capabilities to parse YAML without external dependencies\n- Users have file system access to the YAML files they want to validate\n- YAML files are encoded in UTF-8\n- Single-document YAML files are the primary use case\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Path Input\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer, CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall accept a file path as a command-line argument |\n| **Rationale** | Enables validation of any accessible YAML file in the filesystem |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Command can be invoked with a file path argument (e.g., `bun run validate config.yaml`) <br> - Relative and absolute file paths are supported <br> - Missing file path argument displays usage instructions |\n\n### REQ-002: File Existence Check\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer, CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall verify the specified file exists before attempting validation |\n| **Rationale** | Provides clear error messaging when file is not found |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Non-existent file path produces a clear \"file not found\" error message <br> - Exit code 1 is returned for non-existent files |\n\n### REQ-003: YAML Syntax Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer, CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall determine if the file content is valid, parseable YAML syntax |\n| **Rationale** | Core functionality - enables developers to catch syntax errors early |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Valid YAML files are correctly identified as valid <br> - Invalid YAML syntax is correctly identified as invalid <br> - Common YAML constructs are supported (key-value pairs, nested objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, null) |\n\n### REQ-004: Success Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall display a human-readable success message when YAML is valid |\n| **Rationale** | Provides clear feedback to developers that their file is correctly formatted |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Success message clearly indicates the file is valid <br> - Success message includes the validated file name/path |\n\n### REQ-005: Failure Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall display a human-readable error message when YAML is invalid |\n| **Rationale** | Helps developers understand what is wrong with their YAML file |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Error message clearly indicates the file is invalid <br> - Error message includes relevant details about the parsing failure (e.g., error description from parser) <br> - Error message includes the file name/path that failed |\n\n### REQ-006: Exit Code - Success\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall return exit code 0 when the YAML file is valid |\n| **Rationale** | Enables automated pipelines to programmatically determine validation success |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Process exits with code 0 after successful validation |\n\n### REQ-007: Exit Code - Failure\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall return exit code 1 when the YAML file is invalid or cannot be read |\n| **Rationale** | Enables automated pipelines to programmatically detect validation failures |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - Process exits with code 1 for invalid YAML syntax <br> - Process exits with code 1 for file not found <br> - Process exits with code 1 for file read errors |\n\n### REQ-008: No New Dependencies\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Project Maintainers |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall be implemented without adding new entries to package.json dependencies |\n| **Rationale** | Maintains project simplicity and reduces dependency management burden |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | - No new packages added to dependencies or devDependencies <br> - Implementation uses only Bun built-in capabilities or custom code |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Criteria |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Response time | Validation completes in under 1 second for files up to 10KB |\n| Resource usage | Minimal memory footprint appropriate for CLI tool usage |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | Only read access to the specified file; no write operations |\n| Path traversal | File path is used as-provided; standard OS file access controls apply |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error clarity | Error messages should be understandable without YAML expertise |\n| Usage guidance | Missing or incorrect arguments should display usage help |\n| Consistent output | Message format should be consistent between success and failure cases |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNone identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Configuration File\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | Developer |\n| **I want** | to validate my YAML configuration file from the command line |\n| **So that** | I can catch syntax errors before using the file in my application |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN):**\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file in my project\n- WHEN I run the validate command with the file path\n- THEN I see a clear message indicating whether the file is valid or invalid\n\n### STORY-002: CI Pipeline Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **I want** | to validate YAML files and receive appropriate exit codes |\n| **So that** | I can fail the build when configuration files have syntax errors |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN):**\n- GIVEN a YAML file exists in the repository\n- WHEN the validate command runs as part of CI\n- THEN exit code 0 is returned for valid files, allowing the pipeline to continue\n- AND exit code 1 is returned for invalid files, causing the pipeline to fail\n\n### STORY-003: Identify Syntax Errors\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | Developer |\n| **I want** | to see error details when my YAML file is invalid |\n| **So that** | I can understand and fix the syntax problem |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria (GIVEN/WHEN/THEN):**\n- GIVEN I have a YAML file with a syntax error\n- WHEN I run the validate command\n- THEN I see an error message that describes the parsing failure\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule Description |\n|---------|------------------|\n| BR-001 | A file is considered valid if and only if it can be successfully parsed as YAML |\n| BR-002 | Empty files are considered valid YAML (YAML spec allows empty documents) |\n| BR-003 | Files that cannot be read (permissions, not found) are treated as validation failures |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime | Required to execute the validator command |\n| File system access | System | Required to read YAML files for validation |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No external packages | Must use only Bun built-in capabilities or custom implementation |\n| Single file validation | Only one file can be validated per command invocation |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| What input method should the validator accept? | File path as argument (e.g., `bun run validate config.yaml`) | User clarification |\n| What level of YAML validation is expected? | Syntax validation only (verify it is parseable YAML) | User clarification |\n| What should the output look like? | Simple human-readable pass/fail messages with exit codes (0 for valid, 1 for invalid) | User clarification |\n| Who are the primary users? | Developers and CI/CD pipelines | Inferred from use case |\n| What about stdin or string input? | Out of scope - file path only | Derived from input method clarification |\n| What about schema validation? | Out of scope - syntax only | Derived from validation level clarification |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
