        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-03\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA simple command-line utility that validates YAML files for syntax correctness, enabling users to quickly verify that their YAML configuration files are properly formatted without introducing any new external dependencies to the project.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files need a quick and reliable way to validate file syntax before deployment or use. Currently, there is no built-in mechanism within this project to validate YAML files, forcing users to rely on external tools or discover errors only at runtime.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- **Reduced Errors**: Catch YAML syntax errors early in the development workflow, before they cause runtime failures\n- **Improved Developer Experience**: Provide immediate feedback on YAML file validity without leaving the project toolchain\n- **Zero Dependency Impact**: Maintain the project's minimal dependency footprint by leveraging existing capabilities\n- **Time Savings**: Eliminate context-switching to external validation tools\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement Method |\n|--------|--------|-------------------|\n| Validation Accuracy | 100% detection of syntax errors | Test suite coverage |\n| User Adoption | Command used in CI/CD pipelines | Usage tracking (if applicable) |\n| Zero New Dependencies | 0 new packages added | package.json audit |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| Developer | Primary user writing and maintaining YAML configs | Quick syntax validation during development |\n| CI/CD System | Automated pipeline running validation checks | Non-interactive validation with exit codes |\n| DevOps Engineer | Manages deployment configurations | Batch validation of multiple files |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest | Priority |\n|-------------|----------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain code quality without bloating dependencies | High |\n| Project Maintainers | Keep project lightweight and focused | High |\n| End Users | Reliable validation with clear error messages | High |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Validate YAML syntax for single files\n- Validate YAML syntax for multiple files (batch mode)\n- Report line numbers and positions for syntax errors\n- Provide clear, human-readable error messages\n- Return appropriate exit codes for scripting/CI integration\n- Support reading from stdin for piped input\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating against a schema definition)\n- YAML linting (style enforcement)\n- YAML formatting/pretty-printing\n- YAML transformation or conversion\n- Interactive editing or fixing of YAML files\n- GUI or web interface\n- Adding new external dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime provides sufficient built-in YAML parsing capability or JavaScript-based parsing can be implemented\n- Users have access to command-line interface\n- YAML files are accessible via filesystem or stdin\n- Standard YAML 1.1/1.2 syntax is the target specification\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Single File Validation\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI/CD System\n**Requirement**: The validator shall accept a file path argument and validate the YAML syntax of the specified file.\n**Rationale**: Core functionality - users need to validate individual YAML files.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Given a valid YAML file path, when the validator runs, then it reports success and exits with code 0\n- AC2: Given an invalid YAML file path, when the validator runs, then it reports the syntax error with line number and exits with non-zero code\n- AC3: Given a non-existent file path, when the validator runs, then it reports file not found error and exits with non-zero code\n\n### FR-002: Multiple File Validation\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer, DevOps Engineer\n**Requirement**: The validator shall accept multiple file paths and validate each file, reporting results for all files.\n**Rationale**: Enables batch validation workflows common in deployment scenarios.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Given multiple valid YAML file paths, when the validator runs, then it reports success for each and exits with code 0\n- AC2: Given a mix of valid and invalid files, when the validator runs, then it reports status for each file and exits with non-zero code\n- AC3: Results shall indicate which files passed and which failed\n\n### FR-003: Stdin Support\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer, CI/CD System\n**Requirement**: The validator shall accept YAML content via stdin when no file argument is provided or when explicitly requested.\n**Rationale**: Enables piping and integration with other command-line tools.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Given YAML piped to stdin, when the validator runs with stdin flag, then it validates the piped content\n- AC2: Given invalid YAML via stdin, when the validator runs, then it reports the syntax error\n\n### FR-004: Error Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: All\n**Requirement**: The validator shall report syntax errors with sufficient detail to locate and understand the problem.\n**Rationale**: Users need actionable feedback to fix errors.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Error messages shall include line number where error was detected\n- AC2: Error messages shall include column/position when available\n- AC3: Error messages shall include a description of the syntax problem\n- AC4: Error messages shall be written to stderr\n\n### FR-005: Exit Codes\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: CI/CD System\n**Requirement**: The validator shall return standardized exit codes indicating validation results.\n**Rationale**: Enables scripting and CI/CD integration.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Exit code 0 indicates all files validated successfully\n- AC2: Exit code 1 indicates one or more validation failures\n- AC3: Exit code 2 indicates usage/argument errors\n\n### FR-006: Help/Usage Information\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: All\n**Requirement**: The validator shall display usage information when requested or when invoked incorrectly.\n**Rationale**: Users need guidance on how to use the command.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- AC1: Running with --help or -h flag displays usage information\n- AC2: Usage information includes available options and examples\n- AC3: Running with no arguments and no stdin displays usage hint\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Aspect | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Single File | Validation completes within 100ms for files under 1MB |\n| Startup Time | Command starts and provides feedback within 500ms |\n| Memory | Memory usage proportional to file size, reasonable for typical configs |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Aspect | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| File Access | Only read access required; no file modification |\n| Path Traversal | Validate only explicitly specified paths |\n| No Code Execution | Validator shall not execute any code found in YAML files |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Aspect | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Error Clarity | Error messages understandable without YAML expertise |\n| Consistency | Output format consistent across all validation scenarios |\n| Discoverability | Command follows project's existing CLI patterns |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n| Aspect | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| YAML Specification | Support standard YAML 1.1/1.2 syntax |\n| No New Dependencies | Implementation must use only existing project dependencies |\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Single Config File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate a single YAML configuration file\n**So that** I can catch syntax errors before committing changes\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with valid syntax\n- WHEN I run the validator with the file path\n- THEN I see a success message and the command exits successfully\n\n- GIVEN a YAML file with invalid syntax\n- WHEN I run the validator with the file path\n- THEN I see an error message with line number and the command exits with failure\n\n### STORY-002: Validate Multiple Files in CI\n**As a** CI/CD pipeline\n**I want** to validate all YAML files in a directory\n**So that** deployments are blocked if configuration files have errors\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN multiple YAML files\n- WHEN I run the validator with multiple file paths\n- THEN I see validation results for each file\n- AND the command exits with failure if any file is invalid\n\n### STORY-003: Validate Piped Content\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to pipe YAML content to the validator\n**So that** I can validate generated or transformed YAML\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN YAML content from another command\n- WHEN I pipe it to the validator\n- THEN the content is validated and results are displayed\n\n### STORY-004: Get Help on Usage\n**As a** new user\n**I want** to see how to use the validator\n**So that** I can learn the available options\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN I run the command with --help\n- WHEN the command executes\n- THEN I see usage information with examples\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | Empty files are considered valid YAML | An empty document is valid per YAML spec |\n| BR-002 | Files with only comments are considered valid | Comments-only is valid YAML |\n| BR-003 | Multi-document YAML files shall be fully validated | All documents in a stream must be valid |\n| BR-004 | Binary/non-text files shall be rejected with clear error | Prevent confusing error messages |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun Runtime | Platform | Existing project runtime |\n| Existing package.json deps only | Technical | No new dependencies allowed |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Impact |\n|------------|--------|\n| No new dependencies | Must use built-in or existing parsing capabilities |\n| CLI-only interface | No GUI or web interface |\n| TypeScript codebase | Implementation must follow existing code patterns |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What YAML version to support? | YAML 1.1/1.2 standard syntax | Conservative default |\n| Schema validation needed? | No, syntax validation only | Derived from \"simple\" in requirements |\n| Output format? | Human-readable text to stdout/stderr | Standard CLI convention |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ directory |\n| YAML version | 1.1/1.2 | Industry standard, most compatible |\n| Output format | Human-readable CLI | Matches existing project patterns |\n| Batch support | Included as Should Have | Common CI/CD use case |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" undefined scope | Syntax validation only, no schema/linting | Conservative interpretation of \"simple\" |\n| \"command\" format unclear | CLI subcommand pattern | Existing project structure (src/index.ts CLI pattern) |\n| \"without adding new deps\" | Use Bun built-in or pure JS implementation | Explicit constraint in requirements |\n| Target users not specified | Developers, CI/CD, DevOps | Standard CLI tool user base |\n| Error reporting detail | Line numbers + descriptions | Standard validation tool behavior |\n| Exit code conventions | 0=success, 1=validation fail, 2=usage error | Unix CLI conventions |\n"
