        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/17773350/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator/design-decisions.md",
        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Created**: 2026-02-03\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Pure TypeScript implementation | Zero dependency requirement explicitly stated in requirements | External YAML library (js-yaml, yaml) - rejected due to dependency constraint |\n| D2 | CLI argument parsing | Manual implementation | Zero dependency requirement; validator has simple argument needs | commander.js, yargs - rejected due to dependency constraint |\n| D3 | Command location | `src/commands/yaml-validate/` | Provides modular organization, allows for future commands, keeps related files together | Flat structure in `src/` - rejected as less maintainable for command-specific code |\n| D4 | Test file location | Co-located with source (*.test.ts) | Matches existing project pattern in `src/utils.test.ts` | Separate `test/` directory - rejected to maintain consistency |\n| D5 | Error output destination | stderr | Unix convention for error output, enables stdout to be piped/redirected cleanly | stdout only - rejected as non-conventional |\n| D6 | Exit code scheme | 0 (success), 1 (validation fail), 2 (usage error) | Standard Unix convention, enables precise error handling in scripts/CI | Binary 0/1 - rejected as less informative |\n| D7 | YAML specification version | 1.1/1.2 common subset | Maximum compatibility with real-world YAML files | Strict 1.2 only - rejected as too restrictive |\n| D8 | Module entry point | Separate `cli.ts` for validator | Keeps main project entry point clean, allows direct validator invocation | Integrate into main `src/index.ts` - rejected for separation of concerns |\n| D9 | Stdin detection | Explicit `--stdin` flag | Clear user intent, avoids ambiguity with TTY detection | Auto-detect stdin - rejected as can cause hanging when no input |\n| D10 | Batch output format | Per-file results + summary | Provides both detailed and overview information | Summary only - rejected as less actionable for fixing errors |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| YAML Parser | Pure TypeScript | Requirements constraint | Explicit \"no new dependencies\" requirement in PRD |\n| Test Framework | bun:test | Existing codebase | Project already uses bun:test in utils.test.ts |\n| Module System | ES Modules | Existing codebase | package.json has \"type\": \"module\" |\n| TypeScript Config | Strict mode | Existing codebase | tsconfig.json has \"strict\": true |\n| Script Integration | package.json scripts | Existing codebase | Project uses npm scripts pattern |\n| File Structure | src/ directory | Existing codebase | Existing code lives in src/ |\n| Output Style | Human-readable text | Requirements | CLI tool for developer use |\n\n## Technical Rationale Details\n\n### D1: Pure TypeScript YAML Parser\n\n**Context**: The requirement explicitly states \"without adding new dependencies\" and the package.json constraint confirms this.\n\n**Decision**: Implement a syntax-focused YAML parser in pure TypeScript.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- (+) Zero impact on project dependencies\n- (+) Full control over error messages and positions\n- (+) Can optimize for syntax validation only (faster)\n- (-) More implementation effort than using existing library\n- (-) May not cover all YAML edge cases (acceptable per scope)\n\n**Validation**: Confirmed no YAML parser exists in current dependencies. Bun runtime does not include built-in YAML parsing.\n\n### D2: Manual CLI Argument Parsing\n\n**Context**: CLI libraries like commander.js would add dependencies.\n\n**Decision**: Implement simple argument parsing manually.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- (+) No new dependencies\n- (+) Sufficient for simple flag/path parsing needs\n- (-) Less feature-rich than CLI libraries\n\n**Validation**: The validator's CLI needs are simple: file paths, --help, --stdin, --version. Manual parsing is straightforward.\n\n### D3: Command Directory Structure\n\n**Context**: Need to organize validator code within existing project.\n\n**Decision**: Create `src/commands/yaml-validate/` directory.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- (+) Clear separation of validator code\n- (+) Allows future commands to follow same pattern\n- (+) Related files (types, tests) co-located\n- (-) Slightly deeper directory structure\n\n**Alternative Evaluation**: Flat `src/yaml-validate.ts` was considered but rejected as it doesn't scale well and mixes concerns.\n\n### D7: YAML Version Targeting\n\n**Context**: YAML has multiple specification versions (1.0, 1.1, 1.2) with subtle differences.\n\n**Decision**: Target the common subset of YAML 1.1 and 1.2.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- (+) Maximum compatibility with existing YAML files\n- (+) Covers vast majority of real-world use cases\n- (-) Some edge-case behaviors may differ from strict spec\n\n**Validation**: Most YAML in the wild is compatible with both 1.1 and 1.2 syntax. Strict 1.2 would reject some valid 1.1 files unnecessarily.\n"
